NAME
       sigaction, sigprocmask, sigreturn - install and mask handlers

SYNOPSIS
       #include <signal.h>

       int sigaction(int sig, const struct sigaction *act,
                     struct sigaction *old);
       int sigprocmask(int how, const sigset_t *set, sigset_t *old);

DESCRIPTION
       sigaction installs a disposition for SIG: a handler, SIG_DFL or
       SIG_IGN, together with the mask to apply while the handler runs
       and the flags.

       sigprocmask blocks and unblocks signals, with HOW one of
       SIG_BLOCK, SIG_UNBLOCK or SIG_SETMASK. A signal raised while
       blocked stays pending until it is unblocked.

       Delivery builds a frame on the user stack and returns through
       the trampoline in libc, which calls sigreturn to restore the
       interrupted context. Ordinary C works inside a handler; keep it
       short and touch only volatile sig_atomic_t state.

       A blocking terminal read is interrupted rather than swallowing
       the signal, and fails with EINTR. Full-screen programs use that
       to repaint: the library reports an interrupted read as a resize
       rather than as end of input.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success, -1 with errno set.

ERRORS
       EINVAL   the signal number is not valid, or cannot be caught
       EFAULT   act or old points outside the address space

SEE ALSO
       kill(2), signal(3), setpgid(2), sigtest(1)
